---
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;
const href = (route: string) => `${base}${route.startsWith('/') ? route.slice(1) : route}`;

export const sectionLinks = [
  { id: 'section-o-1-dirac', label: 'O.1 The Dirac Equation' },
  { id: 'section-o-2-spin-orbit', label: 'O.2 The Spin–Orbit Interaction in the Schrödinger Equation' },
  { id: 'section-o-3-atomic-relativity', label: 'O.3 Relativistic Equations and Calculation of the Spin–Orbit Interaction in an Atom' },
];

export const modernPerspective = [
  'Relativistic treatments should be named by level rather than grouped under one label. A scalar-relativistic Hamiltonian retains spin-independent relativistic shifts such as mass–velocity and Darwin effects but does not, by itself, reproduce spin–orbit splitting. A fully relativistic or explicit spinor-SOC calculation contains the spin-dependent coupling needed when the target observable depends on that splitting.',
  'The familiar atomic L·S form is a spherical, atom-centered reduction of the more general momentum–potential-gradient coupling. In a low-symmetry crystal or at an interface, spin need not remain a good quantum number and the relevant SOC matrix elements depend on the actual crystal wavefunctions and symmetry rather than on isolated-atom labels alone.',
  'SOC can change band ordering, open or close gaps, and alter the topology of the occupied subspace. A Rashba-like spin splitting caused by inversion asymmetry is not itself evidence of a topological phase; a topological claim still requires the appropriate bulk gap or nodal structure, symmetry setting, filling, and invariant for the SOC-including Hamiltonian.',
];

export const synthesis =
  'Appendix O supplies the relativistic layer that the main chapters use without repeatedly deriving it. The Dirac equation requires a four-component spinor and places spin and antiparticle structure inside relativistic quantum mechanics. Its low-energy reduction gives the Pauli description with magnetic and spin–orbit terms, while the general SOC operator shows that momentum couples to the gradient of the potential. Near an atom the large radial nuclear field reduces this interaction to an L·σ form, explaining the j = l ± 1/2 splitting and why heavy elements are especially sensitive. The exact spherical Dirac equations then separate, under a small-SOC approximation, into a scalar-relativistic radial problem plus a spin–orbit correction. This distinction connects the atomic discussion of Chapter 10 and relativistic pseudopotentials of Chapter 11 to the SOC-driven band splittings and topological gaps of Chapters 27–28.';
---

<section aria-labelledby="section-o-1-dirac">
  <h2 id="section-o-1-dirac">O.1 The Dirac Equation</h2>
  <p>
    Martin begins from the mismatch between the nonrelativistic Schrödinger equation and special relativity. The Schrödinger equation is first order in time but second order in momentum, whereas a Lorentz-invariant theory must treat space and time consistently. Dirac’s construction makes the equation linear in both time and momentum. The required coefficients cannot be ordinary numbers: they are matrices acting on a four-component wavefunction.
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>i</mi><mi>ℏ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">γ</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">p</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\hbar\frac&#123;\partial\psi&#125;&#123;\partial t&#125;=\left(c\boldsymbol\gamma\cdot\mathbf p+\gamma_0mc^2\right)\psi.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The four components are not an arbitrary enlargement of notation. The relativistic equation contains two spin states and positive- and negative-energy branches; its structure predicts spin one-half and the existence of antiparticles. Electronic-structure calculations at ordinary energies usually do not need to propagate the negative-energy sector explicitly, but the low-energy theory inherits physical terms that cannot be generated by a spin-independent scalar potential alone.
  </p>
  <aside class="callout" aria-label="Relativistic reduction used in electronic structure">
    <p><strong>Relativistic reduction</strong></p>
    <p>four-component Dirac theory → low-energy two-component spinor → scalar-relativistic shifts + magnetic coupling + spin–orbit interaction.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-o-2-spin-orbit">
  <h2 id="section-o-2-spin-orbit">O.2 The Spin–Orbit Interaction in the Schrödinger Equation</h2>
  <p>
    At energies small compared with the electron rest energy, the relativistic theory reduces to a two-component Pauli spinor. In Martin’s notation the familiar Schrödinger kinetic and potential terms are supplemented by a Zeeman term and a spin–orbit term,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="bold">σ</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">B</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>SO</mi></msub><mo>,</mo>
          <mspace width="0.8em"/><mi mathvariant="bold">π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">p</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mi mathvariant="bold">A</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\frac&#123;\boldsymbol\pi^2&#125;&#123;2m&#125;+V+\mu_B\boldsymbol\sigma\cdot\mathbf B+H_&#123;\mathrm&#123;SO&#125;&#125;,\qquad \boldsymbol\pi=\mathbf p-\frac&#123;e&#125;&#123;c&#125;\mathbf A.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The spin–orbit interaction is velocity dependent. Using Martin’s sign and potential convention, its general form is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>H</mi><mi>SO</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>ℏ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">p</mi><mo>×</mo><mo>∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">σ</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_&#123;\mathrm&#123;SO&#125;&#125;=\frac&#123;e\hbar&#125;&#123;4m^2c^2&#125;\left(\mathbf p\times\nabla V\right)\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This form makes the physical origin visible: an electron moving through an electric field experiences, in its rest frame, a magnetic field that couples to its spin. It also shows why explicit SOC generally mixes the two spin components. Treating ↑ and ↓ as independent scalar channels is therefore an additional approximation unless symmetry makes that separation exact.
  </p>
  <p>
    In solids the strongest atomic contribution is usually generated close to a nucleus, where the potential changes rapidly. For a spherical potential the gradient is radial and the operator reduces to an angular-momentum form,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>H</mi><mi>SO</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac>
          <mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant="bold">L</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">σ</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_&#123;\mathrm&#123;SO&#125;&#125;=\frac&#123;\hbar^2&#125;&#123;4M^2c^2&#125;\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;r&#125;\frac&#123;dV&#125;&#123;dr&#125;\mathbf L\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The eigenstates then organize according to total angular momentum, producing the familiar j = l ± 1/2 splitting. This atomic form carries naturally into atom-centered basis and augmentation methods; relativistic atomic calculations can also build the corresponding angular-momentum dependence into pseudopotentials, as discussed in <a href={href('/reading/books/martin/chapter-11/')}>Chapter 11</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Martin also emphasizes planar geometries. At a surface or interface the absence of inversion symmetry permits a momentum-dependent spin splitting that is linear near suitable states—the setting associated with Rashba-type physics. The same relativistic interaction is present, but the relevant angular momentum is now generated by the asymmetric crystal environment rather than by an isolated spherical atom.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-o-3-atomic-relativity">
  <h2 id="section-o-3-atomic-relativity">O.3 Relativistic Equations and Calculation of the Spin–Orbit Interaction in an Atom</h2>
  <p>
    The final section returns to the full Dirac equation for a spherical atomic potential. Conservation of parity and total angular momentum allows the four-component state to be separated into radial functions and coupled orbital–spin angular functions. The two radial components have opposite parity and are associated with the two possibilities j = l ± 1/2.
  </p>
  <p>
    Martin rewrites the exact radial equations using an energy- and radius-dependent effective mass,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>M</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(r)=m+\frac&#123;\varepsilon-V(r)&#125;&#123;2c^2&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Up to this point no scalar-relativistic approximation has been made. Relative to an ordinary radial Schrödinger equation, the exact equation contains the varying effective mass together with additional Darwin and spin–orbit terms. This is the useful conceptual decomposition: some relativistic effects shift levels without coupling spin directions, whereas the SOC term carries the explicit spin–angular-momentum dependence.
  </p>
  <p>
    Martin then introduces the approximation used widely in electronic structure when SOC is small enough to treat separately. The SOC contribution is omitted while solving the radial functions, leaving a <strong>scalar-relativistic</strong> equation that retains the spin-independent relativistic corrections. SOC can then be added using matrix elements of the angular-momentum-dependent operator. The approximation is valuable because the scalar radial problem can be solved with methods close to the ordinary Schrödinger atom while retaining the dominant relativistic change of the radial states.
  </p>
  <aside class="callout" aria-label="Relativistic levels in Martin's construction">
    <p><strong>Relativistic levels in Martin’s construction</strong></p>
    <p>full spherical Dirac equation → exact coupled radial components → omit SOC only when justified → scalar-relativistic radial states → add SOC coupling for the target solid or molecule.</p>
  </aside>
  <p>
    This hierarchy explains several earlier parts of the book. <a href={href('/reading/books/martin/chapter-10/')}>Chapter 10</a> uses the atomic picture to motivate relativistic effects in heavy atoms; <a href={href('/reading/books/martin/chapter-11/')}>Chapter 11</a> transfers scalar and spin–orbit information through relativistic pseudopotentials; and <a href={href('/reading/books/martin/chapter-27/')}>Chapters 27</a>–<a href={href('/reading/books/martin/chapter-28/')}>28</a> show cases where even a comparatively small SOC term changes the qualitative band topology by opening or reorganizing gaps.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the broader prerequisite map, see <a href={href('/theory/relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism/')}>Relativistic Electronic Structure, Spin, and Magnetism</a>.
  </p>
</section>
